{"kind":"request","text":"Claude: a batch preflight must read the log once, not once per labelled act.\n\nhugh asked on 2026-10-16 why landing in the artroom workroom still takes so long, and approved filing this.\n\nMeasured on 2026-10-16 by replaying artroom's public item-query landing (approval 8e5af11e, 58 successors and 104 retirements, log depth 43,744) on a frozen copy, with gs built from main 5876d211 and a wall-clock stack sampler. The replay took 1,313 s. It started 179 full-log `git rev-list --first-parent --reverse` scans at about 6.8 s each, and the sampler puts about 1,070 s under kernel.PriorAct. In the real landing, main moved at 09:22:14 and the merge receipt was appended at 09:36:30. 119 scans × 6.8 s ≈ 13.5 min, which matches that gap.\n\nThe cause: preflightAdmission (cmd/gs/main.go:2362@5876d211) calls Workspace.AcceptedActUnderKey for every labelled act (cmd/gs/main.go:2397@5876d211). That calls kernel.PriorAct, which runs a fresh, uncached, signature-verified scanHead of the whole log (internal/kernel/kernel.go:477@5876d211). A merge runs the preflight three times over the same acts: once before moving the target (cmd/gs/main.go:1050@5876d211), once in recordMergeSuccession (cmd/gs/succession.go:170@5876d211), and once inside runBatch (cmd/gs/main.go:2304@5876d211, called from cmd/gs/succession.go:175@5876d211). The cost is depth × labelled acts × 3, so every landing gets slower as the log grows. It came in with 82f612df1 (2026-09-06).\n\nOutcome. One preflight of one batch answers every key from one verified read of the log. The same accepted-act answers come back as today, from the same dedup identity Submit consults. A merge runs the preflight over its succession once after the target moves, not twice. Whatever the fix, each key must still be answered from a verified log at the frontier being judged.","body":{"conditions":"1. A batch preflight of N labelled acts performs at most one verified log scan to answer every key; a test counts the scans (or PriorAct-equivalent reads) and goes red when the per-act lookup returns. 2. A merge runs the succession preflight once after the target moves, not twice; a test pins the count. 3. Replay behaviour is unchanged: a label whose key the log already holds resolves to that event, a reused key over a different intent is still refused, and the existing replay tests pass unmodified. 4. The answer still comes from a verified log at the frontier being judged; no unverified cache is introduced. 5. go test ./... passes at the head. 6. The report states, from a replay of the same artroom landing on a frozen copy, the before and after wall time and full-log scan count, or says plainly what was not measured.","target_head":"5876d2118fa3d9d223dfc923744bc0bb9ef1dfc0","target_ref":"refs/heads/main","target_repo":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8","to":"a5d35aa7e4799472a208663d7f024917442bd513929f9eccf450628ccf70095d"}}